.TH ANKI\-TOOLS "1" "2026\-11\-11" "anki\-tools" "anki\-tools"
.SH NAME
anki\-tools \- version control for Anki deck packages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B anki\-tools
[-h] COMMAND ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBanki\-tools\fR is for collaborating on Anki decks in version control. A
deck is normally a binary package (.apkg), so git cannot show what changed or
merge two versions. \fBanki\-tools\fR converts a deck to plain text files
instead, which diff and merge like any other source. \fBdump\fR writes the
text, \fBdiff\fR compares two versions, and \fBbuild\fR recreates a package.
.PP
Reach for it when a deck is shared between people, or published from a
repository, where every change becomes a review, and CI can build the .apkg to
ship. The intended cycle is in \fBWORKFLOW\fR below.

.SH
COMMANDS
.SS \fBanki\-tools dump\fR
extract a deck package into a dump

usage: anki\-tools dump [options] PACKAGE

Extract a deck package into a dump (directory or .zip).

arguments:
.RS 7
.TP
\fBpackage\fR
path to an .apkg file
.RE


options:
.RS 7
.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fI\,OUTPUT\/\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI\,OUTPUT\/\fR
output directory or file ("\-" for stdout)

.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fI\,{jsonl,zip}\/\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI\,{jsonl,zip}\/\fR
output format (default: inferred from the output path)
.RE


export options
.RS 7
.TP
\fB\-q\fR \fI\,QUERY\/\fR, \fB\-\-query\fR \fI\,QUERY\/\fR
Anki search selecting which notes to include

.TP
\fB\-\-with\-deck\-configs\fR
include deck presets

.TP
\fB\-\-no\-media\fR
exclude media files
.RE

.SS \fBanki\-tools build\fR
build an .apkg from a dump

usage: anki\-tools build [options] SOURCE

Build a deck package from a dump (directory or .zip).

arguments:
.RS 7
.TP
\fBsource\fR
dump directory or file ("\-" for stdin)
.RE


options:
.RS 7
.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fI\,OUTPUT\/\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI\,OUTPUT\/\fR
output .apkg path ("\-" for stdout)

.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fI\,{jsonl,zip}\/\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI\,{jsonl,zip}\/\fR
input format (default: inferred from the source path)

.TP
\fB\-\-update\-deck\-presets\fR \fI\,{never,always,on\-change}\/\fR
how the package picks deck preset ids: never keeps the dump's id (default),
always mints a new one, on\-change follows the content
.RE

.SS \fBanki\-tools diff\fR
compare two packages or dumps

usage: anki\-tools diff [options] OLD NEW

Compare two packages or dumps and print a unified diff. Exit codes follow the diff utility: 0 when they match, 1 when they differ, 2 on an error. A dump records the export options it was made with; the two inputs must agree on them, or the diff would report scope and preset differences as content changes. The export options only affect how a package is read; \-e/\-\-exclude drops a section from the comparison itself and works for any input. Only changed lines are shown by default; \-U/\-\-context adds lines around each change.

arguments:
.RS 7
.TP
\fBold\fR
baseline package, dump or "\-"

.TP
\fBnew\fR
changed package, dump or "\-"
.RE


options:
.RS 7
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fI\,{text,markdown}\/\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI\,{text,markdown}\/\fR
output format (default: text)

.TP
\fB\-o\fR \fI\,OUTPUT\/\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fI\,OUTPUT\/\fR
output file ("\-" for stdout)

.TP
\fB\-e\fR \fI\,SECTION\/\fR, \fB\-\-exclude\fR \fI\,SECTION\/\fR
section to drop from the diff (repeatable: notetypes, decks, deck_configs,
notes, media)

.TP
\fB\-U\fR \fI\,N\/\fR, \fB\-\-context\fR \fI\,N\/\fR
lines of context around each change (default: 0)

.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR, \fB\-\-no\-color\fR
colourise text output (default: auto)

.TP
\fB\-\-no\-warnings\fR
hide the re\-import warnings (errors are still printed)
.RE


export options
.RS 7
.TP
\fB\-q\fR \fI\,QUERY\/\fR, \fB\-\-query\fR \fI\,QUERY\/\fR
Anki search selecting which notes to include

.TP
\fB\-\-with\-deck\-configs\fR
include deck presets

.TP
\fB\-\-no\-media\fR
exclude media files
.RE

.SS \fBanki\-tools inspect\fR
summarize a deck package

usage: anki\-tools inspect [options] PACKAGE

Summarize a deck package.

arguments:
.RS 7
.TP
\fBpackage\fR
path to an .apkg file
.RE


options:
.RS 7
.TP
\fB\-f\fR \fI\,{text,json}\/\fR, \fB\-\-format\fR \fI\,{text,json}\/\fR
output format (default: text)
.RE

.SH WORKFLOW
A dump is a generated artifact, not an editing surface. Your Anki collection
is the source of truth.
.IP "1." 4
Change the deck in Anki and export an .apkg.
.IP "2." 4
Run \fBanki\-tools dump deck.apkg \-o deck/\fR and commit the text.
.IP "3." 4
Review and merge the dump in git like any other text.
.IP "4." 4
Let CI run \fBanki\-tools build deck/ \-o deck.apkg\fR to produce the release
package.
.PP
The text format exists so changes are reviewable and mergeable, not so you
author content in it. To change what a card says, change it in Anki and dump
again.
.PP
\fBbuild\fR either reproduces a dump exactly or fails with a message; it never
silently renames, collapses or drops content. A dump that a git merge produced
by hand is the case this protects you from: if \fBbuild\fR refuses one, fix the
duplicate name or reference in Anki and dump again.
.PP
Where the source .apkg lives, whether committed, a release asset or nowhere at
all, is up to you. anki\-tools does not care.
.PP
A dump is written to a staging directory beside the target and published only
once it is complete, so an interrupted dump leaves the previous dump in place
rather than a truncated file. Only a hard kill (SIGKILL) can leave a hidden
\&.dump.XXXX staging directory behind, and it is safe to delete.
.PP
Publishing replaces the dump's own files and its media/ directory. Anything
else you keep in that directory is left alone.
.SH NOTES
A dump carries every option Anki stores for note types, decks, presets and
notes (field fonts and sizes, template browser formats, deck descriptions), so
a rebuilt package keeps them.
.PP
The export options only affect how a package is read. \fB\-e/\-\-exclude\fR
drops a section from the comparison itself and works for any input, so
\fB\-e media\fR is how you ignore media differences, including between two
dumps. \fB\-\-no\-media\fR has no effect when both inputs are dumps.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS 4
.nf
anki\-tools dump deck.apkg \-o deck/                    # canonical, diffable
anki\-tools dump deck.apkg > deck.zip                  # single\-file archive
anki\-tools dump deck.apkg \-o deck/ \-q 'deck:Latein'   # select a subset
anki\-tools build deck/ \-o deck.apkg
anki\-tools build deck.zip \-o \- > deck.apkg           # to stdout
anki\-tools diff old.apkg new.apkg
anki\-tools diff old/ new.apkg \-f markdown
anki\-tools inspect deck.apkg \-\-format json
.fi
.RE
.SH IMPORT LIMITATIONS
Anki's package import decides what a re\-import carries, and dump and build
cannot change that. \fBdiff\fR warns about the rows marked "no", naming the
entity and linking a tracking issue. It also reports a note type that shares a
name but not an id, since a re\-import then adds a duplicate. The notes go to
standard error, so the diff on standard output is unaffected;
\fB\-\-no\-warnings\fR hides the warnings and keeps the errors.

.TS
tab(;);
l l.
Change;Re-import applies
_
note values, tags;yes (package must be newer)
template content, CSS;yes
note type / field / template name;yes (ids are carried)
field or template added;with merge + always update
field or template removed;no (issue #9)
field or template reordered;no (issue #10)
card moved to another deck;no (issue #7)
deck renamed;no, cards stay in old deck (#7)
deck preset changed;no (issue #8)
.TE
.PP
To apply an added field, import the package with "merge note types" and
"always update" ticked. The cost is that local edits are overwritten; card
scheduling is kept.
.PP
A preset change cannot be updated in place. \fB\-\-update\-deck\-presets\fR
decides what the built package does about it: \fBnever\fR (the default) keeps
the dump's id, so nothing duplicates and a change does not reach an importer;
\fBalways\fR mints a new id, so a change arrives as a second preset and the old
one stays; \fBon\-change\fR derives the id from the preset's content, so an
unchanged preset does not duplicate and a changed one arrives as a new preset.
Either way the learner may need to delete the stale preset by hand.
.PP
The \fBIMPORT LIMITATIONS\fR above are tracked as
.UR https://github.com/lsg551/anki-tools/issues?q=is%3Aissue+label%3A%22import+limitation%22
issues tagged "import limitation"
.UE .
.SH CAVEATS
.IP \(bu 4
\fBIdentity is by name within anki\-tools.\fR Decks, note types, presets and
media files are matched by name when a dump is validated and diffed, so two
entities with the same name cannot be told apart, and dump and build refuse
them instead of merging. The built package carries note type and preset ids,
which Anki matches by id on import; decks and media files it matches by name,
so renaming a deck or a media file reads as a delete and an add.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBThe built\-in Default preset and the empty Default deck are not dumped.\fR
A package is imported into a fresh collection, which always has both, so they
are not content. Anki's own exporter behaves the same: it drops empty decks and
never round\-trips the built\-in preset's settings.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBBuilds are not byte\-reproducible.\fR Import assigns fresh deck, note and
card IDs and stamps timestamps, so two builds of the same dump differ in bytes
even though the content is identical. Note type IDs, and preset IDs unless
\fB\-\-update\-deck\-presets\fR is \fBalways\fR or \fBon\-change\fR, are
reproduced. Therefore always compare dumps, not \&.apkg bytes.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBScheduling information is not part of a dump.\fR dump and build are
content\-only: card scheduling (due, interval, reviews), the review log, and
deck\-level scheduling and UI state (per\-deck limits, today's counters,
collapsed state) are never included. This is deliberate: it changes on every
review, so storing it would make every dump churn and defeat diffing. Anki's
export option to include scheduling is therefore not mirrored by dump, and
inspect reports whether a package carries personal learning state.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBNotes with no cards are not rebuilt.\fR If all of a note's cards were
deleted in Anki, the note stays in the dump but is skipped on build, because
Anki cannot create a note without cards. dump warns when this happens.
.IP \(bu 4
\fBMedia is the only binary part of a dump\fR (stored as files under media/, or
inside the .zip archive). Everything else is text. A dump keeps the media Anki's
exporter would keep: files referenced by note fields, plus the _\-prefixed files
a note type uses in its templates or CSS. Images referenced from a template or
a deck description without that prefix are not exported by Anki either, so they
are not part of a dump.

